t:nonroot"`, `requiredCopyChown = "--chown=nonroot:nonroot"`, `requiredBuilderBuildFlags = {CGO_ENABLED=0, -trimpath, -s -w}`. Closed sets for forbidden patterns: `forbiddenSecretEnvFragments = {TOKEN, PASSWORD, SECRET, API_KEY, PRIVATE_KEY, SIGNING_KEY, DSN}` (case-insensitive substring on ENV key), `forbiddenRuntimePackageInstalls = {apt-get, apt install, yum install, dnf install, apk add, microdnf install}` (substring on runtime stage's joined RUN payload). The closed-set design is deliberate — it catches the secret-shaped fragments the project's config layer actually uses, not every possible English word that contains "key", so a future ENV that holds a non-secret hint does not false-positive. (4) SECURITY.md is the human-facing co-contract: the same test file pins (a) a verification-gate row in the Required Verification Gates table AND (b) a dedicated `## Container Image Hardening` section with literal substrings (`distroless`, `nonroot`, `Multi-stage build`, `CGO_ENABLED=0`, `-trimpath`, `Container image hardening`). A regression that drops either is caught as deterministically as a Dockerfile regression. (5) The postgres image pin regex `^postgres:\d+$` deliberately rejects variant tags like `postgres:16-bookworm` because the local integration-test stack must remain reproducible across local environments; if a future story needs a variant the regex must widen in lockstep with the fixture catalogue. (6) Builder stage vs runtime stage have ASYMMETRIC rules: builder stages may install packages (`apt-get install git` for the goreleaser-style git tag lookup is legitimate), the runtime stage may not. The matcher pins the asymmetry by walking only the runtime stage's instructions for the package-install scan and only the builder stage's instructions for the build-flag scan. The runtime stage is identified as the LAST stage (the unnamed final FROM); the builder stage as the FIRST stage with an `AS` name. Five test-fixture gotchas: (a) when SECURITY.md and the matcher